 You will find [it] easily: get out of the city through the Aix Gate.\par 7.\emdash  Continue straight up to the third street on the left\u8230\'c9\par 8.\emdash  Go along this street for thirty meters (}{\i\f211 make a-journey through this street three-hundred meters}{\f208 )\u8230\'c9\par 9.\emdash  There, to the right, you will see a house with a red door which won't be fully closed.\par 10.\emdash  You will push the door with [your] foot and you will enter.\par 11.\emdash  Why with [my] foot?\par 12.\emdash  Because [your] hands will be full\u8230\'c9\par 13.\emdash  \u8230\'c9of the presents that without any doubt you will bring us.\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1.}{\f208  }{\b\f210 In }{\f208 u}{\b\f210 rbe Mass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 li\u225\'87}{\f208 , and not }{\b\f210 Mass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 liae}{\f208 , since, due to the fact that }{\b\f210 Mass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lia}{\f208  is linked by it agreement to u}{\b\f210 rbe}{\f208 , one cannot use anymore the shorter construction }{\b\f210 Mass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 liae}{\f208  (that the grammarians call locative) which allowes one to say in just one word: }{\i\f211 in Marseille}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 2.}{\f208  A}{\b\f210 mbulans}{\f208  (plural }{\b\f210 ambul}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ntes}{\f208 ), present participle of a}{\b\f210 mbulo}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 (}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 mbul)as}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 (ambul)}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am walking}{\f208 . From now on, we shall offer you the verbs in shortened form in the order: }{\i\f211 1}{\i\f211\super st}{\i\f211  person present indicative}{\f208  (not shortened); }{\i\f211 ending of the 2}{\i\f211\super nd}{\i\f211  person}{\f208  (here }{\b\f210 as}{\f208 ); }{\i\f211 ending of the present infinitive}{\f208  (here a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 3.}{\f208  }{\b\f210 M}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 rce}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 M}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ri}{\f208 , and in the preceding lesson o}{\b\f210 ptime Fabr}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ci}{\f208 , are }{\b\f210